# Secrets - including backup copies like .env.bak
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Your content: layout, symbols, spoken sentences, written by app.py when
# there was an app.py. Deliberately not versioned - the names, photos and
# sentences of your child were in here. The browser keeps all of it in
# IndexedDB now, so this only matters for a clone that predates that.
#
# **Anchored, and that is not tidiness.** These were written as bare `content`
# and `data`, which match a file or directory of that name *at any depth* - so
# the moment the front end grew a data/ directory holding the format modules, five
# files stopped being offered up for committing and nobody was told. They were
# on disk, every local check passed, and CI failed on a tree that did not have
# them. The leading slash pins each to the root, where the thing being ignored
# actually was.
/content

# The same content again, seen from a container that no longer exists:
# docker-compose.yml handed data/ in as /data, so this is where it landed when
# the container was run from a clone.
/data

# arduino-cli puts its intermediate directory inside the sketch folder
firmware/vorlaut/build/
# the same for the test stages
firmware/tests/*/build/
# where --output-dir collects the finished images, as CI and the release do.
# An 8 MB merged.bin is easy to commit by accident and hard to get back out.
build/

# What the firmware gets is recreated from content/ in seconds
firmware/vorlaut/data/
# layout.h is no longer produced, but may be lying around from older states -
# and it held the sentences in plain text.
firmware/vorlaut/layout.h
firmware/vorlaut/littlefs.bin


# Python
# Also without a trailing slash, and for a reason that turned up in practice:
# a worktree that symlinks .venv to the one next door - so that .venv/bin/python
# keeps working there - had that symlink staged by git add -A.
.venv
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# What tools/ttscheck.py renders for the browser to level and hand back.
# Twenty WAVs three times over, none of them worth keeping.
/dump/

# What npm installs. The lockfile is what is committed - it pins every one of
# these, including the two @lautstark packages, which are git dependencies
# resolved to a commit rather than a registry version.
node_modules/

# What Vite builds. Uploaded by the Pages workflow and never committed: a
# checked-in build output is a second copy of the front end that goes stale
# quietly, and every check here runs against loader/ rather than against it.
dist/

# Playwright's own leavings.
test-results/
playwright-report/

# tsc -b's incremental state, which lives under node_modules/.tmp - listed
# because the path is not obvious and somebody will go looking for it.
node_modules/.tmp/

# tsc -b leaves this at the root for the solution file itself.
tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
shots/
